''throat.'' A similar notion appears in the origin of a diff. noun of the same sense, namely D. ''stro'' = OFries. ''strot'' (''-bolla'') = MLG. ''strote'' = MHG. ''strozze'' (> It. ''strozza''), the throat, gullet; from the root of ''strut'', 'swell,' be prominent.] 1. The front of the neck below the chin and above the collar-bone; technically, the jugular region, jugulum, or guttur.

{{smaller block|I prithee, take thy fingers from my ''throat.''
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, v. 1. 283.}}}}

2. The passage from the mouth to the stomach or to the lungs. {{smaller|(''a'') The swallow or gullet; technically, the fauces, pharynx, and esophagus.}}

{{smaller block|And thei duellen alle weye in Roches or in Mountaynes; and thei han alle wey the ''Throte'' open, of whens thei droppen Venym alle weys.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 290.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') The air-passage in the throat; the windpipe; technically, the larynx and trachea: as, to form musical notes in the ''throat.''}}

{{smaller block|I'll have you preferred to be a crier; you have an excellent ''throat'' for 't.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, iii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|The cock, that is the trumpet to the morn,
Doth with his lofty and shrill-sounding ''throat''
Awake the god of day.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 1. 151.}}}}

3. Something resembling or analogous to the human throat. {{smaller|(''a'') In ''entom.'', the gula, or posterior part of the lower side of the head, behind the mentum. (''b'') In ''bot.'', the mouth or orifice of a gamopetalous corolla or calyx,}}

readings.c2s1:
brasure (which see). (''m'') In ''angling'', a straitened body of water flowing with a smooth current through a narrow place, as between rocks in a river.

{{smaller block|Some men fish a ''throat'' by the simple resource of keeping the point of the rod steady at an angle above the cast and letting the current itself take the fly round.
{{right|''Quarterly Rev.'', CXXVI. 348.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Almond of the throat.''' See ''almond.''—'''Clergyman's sore throat.''' See ''clergyman.''—'''Sore throat,''' inflammation of the lining membrane of the gullet, pharynx, fauces, or upper air-passages, attended by pain on swallowing.—'''To cut one another's throat,''' figuratively, to engage, as two dealers, in a ruinous competition. [Colloq.]—'''To cut one's own throat,''' figuratively, to adopt a suicidal policy. [Colloq.]—'''To give one the lie in his throat†.''' See ''give.''—'''To have a bone in one's throat.''' See ''bone''<sup>1</sup>.—'''To lie in one's throat.''' See ''lie''<sup>2</sup>.}}

'''throat''' (thrōt) ''v. t.'' [< ''throat'', ''n.''] 1†. To utter in a guttural tone; mutter.

{{smaller block|So Hector hereto ''throated'' threats to go to sea in blood.
{{right|''Chapman'', Iliad, xiii. 135.}}}}

2. To channel or groove.

{{smaller block|Sills are weathered and ''throated'' like the parts of a string course.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', IV. 472.}}}}

'''throatal†''', ''n.'' A corrupt spelling of ''throttle.''

'''throat-band''' (thrōt′band), ''n.'' A band about the throat; specifically, the throat-latch of a bridle. See cut under ''harness.''

'''throat-boll†''' (thrōt′bōl), ''n.'' [< ME. ''throtebolle'', < AS. ''throtbolla'' (cf. OFries. ''strotbolla''), the throat, < ''throtu'', throat, + ''bolla'', a round object: see ''bowl''<sup>2</sup>. Cf. ''thropple.''] The protuberance in the throat called Adam's apple; hence, the throat itself.

{{smaller block|By the ''throte bolle'' he caughte Aleyn.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Reeve's Tale, l. 353.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|Yet my heart<br>''Throbs'' to know one thing.
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, iv. 1. 101.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Throbbing'', as ''throbs'' the bosom, hot and fast.
{{right|''Lowell'', Ode to France, viii.}}}}

2. To quiver or vibrate.

{{smaller block|Till the war-drum ''throbb'd'' no longer, and the battle-flags were furl'd<br>In the Parliament of men, the Federation of the world.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Locksley Hall.}}}}

¶
'''throb''' (throb), ''n.'' [< ''throb'', ''v.''] A beat or strong pulsation; a violent beating, as of the heart and arteries; a palpitation: as, a ''throb'' of pleasure or of pain.

{{smaller block|There an huge heap of singults did oppresse<br>His strugling soule, and swelling ''throbs'' empeach<br>His foltring toung with pangs of drerinesse.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., III. xi. 11.}}}}

{{smaller block|Endeavors for freedom are animating; nor can any honest nature hear of them without a ''throb'' of sympathy.
{{right|''Sumner'', Orations, I. 239.}}}}

'''throbbant†''', ''a.'' [ME., ppr. of ''throb''.] Throbbing.

{{smaller block|And thanne I kneled on my knes and kyste her wel sone,<br>And thanked hure a thousand sythes with ''throbbant'' herte.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (A), xii. 48.}}}}

'''throbbingly''' (throb′ing-li), ''adv.'' In a throbbing manner; with throbs or pulsations.

'''throbless''' (throb′les), ''a.'' [< ''throb'' + ''-less''.] Not beating or throbbing. [Rare.]
